    "title": "Django DisallowedHost page in a response",
    "description": "A Django DisallowedHost error page appears in the response body. The page prints the exact Host header the server rejected and the list of allowed hosts, which tells an attacker which virtual hosts the deployment answers to.",
    "remediation": "Set ALLOWED_HOSTS explicitly, serve a generic error page and keep DEBUG off in production.",
    "limitation": "One response body. It matches the Django host-rejection page, not other Django errors.",
    "scopeT": "One response body. It matches the Django host-rejection page, not other Django errors.",
    "fp": "A documentation page that quotes the message is still reported; confirm it is static text.",
    "recordDesc": "A Django DisallowedHost error page in a response gives every reader the same view the bank has internally: The page prints the exact Host header the server rejected and the list of allowed hosts, which tells an attacker which virtual hosts the deployment answers to.",
    "applicability": "Django applications with DEBUG on or an over-broad ALLOWED_HOSTS wildcard.",
    "risk": "The allowed-host list maps the estate and reveals administrative virtual hosts.",
    "why": "The rejected value and the allowlist together describe the routing table of the deployment.",
    "fix": [
      "Turn DEBUG off and set ALLOWED_HOSTS to the exact production names.",
      "Return a generic 400 page for an unrecognised Host header."
    ],
